  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\n- Scattered Showers and Isolated Storms This Morning\n\nRegional composite radar imagery shows convection developing \nassociated with a Low-level jet overrunning the stationary front to \nour south. The best coverage will be south, however rain showers are \npossible into this morning, especially south of I96. Instability is \npretty marginal but cannot rule out isolated storms. Conditions then \ndry out later today into Friday as high pressure takes over as the \nmain driver of our weather.\n\n- Heavy Rain Possible This Saturday\n\nOn the heels of this breif dry window comes widespread precipitation \nFriday Night into Saturday. A ample southerly LLJ forces the front \nsouth of us back north as a warm front. In conjunction with marginal \ninstability and PWATs near 2\" this is a favorable setup for high \nrainfall efficiency. Some question based on low placemenet and \nconvective track as to where exactly the heaviest rain falls, \nhowever heavy rain is likely. High confidence in rainfall north of \n1\" across much of the area, with NBM probabilities of > 2\" north of \n60% in spots. Overall the slight ERO from WPC remains in good shape.",
